HID: core: register hidraw before hidinput

The bluez sixaxis plugin does the PS3 cable pairing from userspace: on the
hidraw udev event it reads the pad's own Bluetooth address with a feature
report ioctl and writes the host address back. It only gets to do that while
the pad is still on the USB bus.

Some PS3 clones give a very short window. Measured on a S905X3 box with a
clone that announces itself as "Nintendo Co., Ltd. USB Gamepad" (054C:0268):
the pad re-enumerates itself as 045E:028E (X-input) 120 ms after it appears,
every single time.

hid_connect() runs hidinput_connect() first, and for these pads that calls
sony_input_configured(), which serialises two GET_REPORT feature transfers,
the motion sensor input device, four LED classdevs, the battery probe with
hid_hw_open() and force feedback setup - all before hidraw_connect() gets to
register the char device. That pushes the hidraw node out to ~91 ms and
leaves bluez ~25 ms of the pad's 120 ms window:

  hid-sony      enum -> hidraw 91.3 ms (mean of 8)  ->  25 ms left
  hid-generic   enum -> hidraw 27.0 ms (mean of 5)  ->  94 ms left

  bluez cable pairing succeeded 2 of 8 times with hid-sony, 5 of 5 with
  hid-generic

Without the pairing record bluez then refuses the pad's incoming Bluetooth
connection ("Refusing connection: unknown device"), so the pad has to be
paired by hand through the Bluetooth menu - which is not an option on a box
whose settings are locked.

hidraw_connect() only allocates a minor and creates the char device. It does
not touch hdev->claimed for input, hidinput, or any hid_hw_* state, so it can
run first. Doing so hands the hidraw node to userspace at hid-generic speed
while keeping hid-sony bound, with its LEDs, rumble and sensors intact.

Two consequences, both benign:
  - HID_CLAIMED_HIDRAW is set before hidinput_connect() runs, so an input
    report arriving in between reaches hidraw readers. hidinput_report_event()
    is still skipped because HID_CLAIMED_INPUT is not set yet.
  - The hidrawN uevent now precedes the inputN one. No batocera udev rule
    looks up an input sibling from a hidraw event.

hid_disconnect() tears down in the order hidinput, hiddev, hidraw and does not
need to change. The "input,hiddev96,hidraw0" line is built from the claimed
mask in a fixed order, so its text is unchanged.

diff --git a/drivers/hid/hid-core.c b/drivers/hid/hid-core.c
--- a/drivers/hid/hid-core.c
+++ b/drivers/hid/hid-core.c
@@ -2190,6 +2190,14 @@ int hid_connect(struct hid_device *hdev, unsigned int connect_mask)
 	if (hid_hiddev(hdev))
 		connect_mask |= HID_CONNECT_HIDDEV_FORCE;
 
+	/*
+	 * Register hidraw first: it is cheap, and a driver's ->input_configured
+	 * can be slow enough (hid-sony on a USB Sixaxis) that userspace misses a
+	 * short-lived device entirely.
+	 */
+	if ((connect_mask & HID_CONNECT_HIDRAW) && !hidraw_connect(hdev))
+		hdev->claimed |= HID_CLAIMED_HIDRAW;
+
 	if ((connect_mask & HID_CONNECT_HIDINPUT) && !hidinput_connect(hdev,
 				connect_mask & HID_CONNECT_HIDINPUT_FORCE))
 		hdev->claimed |= HID_CLAIMED_INPUT;
@@ -2198,8 +2206,6 @@ int hid_connect(struct hid_device *hdev, unsigned int connect_mask)
 			!hdev->hiddev_connect(hdev,
 				connect_mask & HID_CONNECT_HIDDEV_FORCE))
 		hdev->claimed |= HID_CLAIMED_HIDDEV;
-	if ((connect_mask & HID_CONNECT_HIDRAW) && !hidraw_connect(hdev))
-		hdev->claimed |= HID_CLAIMED_HIDRAW;
 
 	if (connect_mask & HID_CONNECT_DRIVER)
 		hdev->claimed |= HID_CLAIMED_DRIVER;
